WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.980
Most LLM inference can be understood as two related phases: prefill and decode.

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:07.930
Prefill is the work of reading the prompt and existing context.

00:00:07.930 --> 00:00:12.220
If the user sends a long document, a repository excerpt, or a large instruction block,

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:15.020
prefill can dominate the time before the model starts speaking.

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:18.925
Decode is the phase that produces new tokens after the prompt has been processed.

00:00:18.925 --> 00:00:21.450
Batching often helps these phases in different ways.

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:22.340
During prefill,

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:26.400
the runtime may be able to process larger blocks of prompt tokens efficiently because

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:28.050
there is a lot of parallel work available.

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:28.860
During decode,

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:32.260
each active sequence usually needs the next token before it can move forward,

00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:34.020
so the shape of the work is more incremental.

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:37.820
That is why prompt processing speed and generation speed should be measured separately.

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:41.605
First-token latency is the time between submitting the request and seeing the first

00:00:41.605 --> 00:00:42.500
generated token.

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:45.980
It is strongly affected by prompt length, queueing, prefill efficiency,

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:47.535
and how many other requests are active.

00:00:47.535 --> 00:00:51.151
Tokens per second measures generation speed once the model is producing output.

00:00:51.151 --> 00:00:55.610
Requests per second matters more for services that handle many users or automated clients.

00:00:55.610 --> 00:00:59.710
A workload made of long prompts and short answers behaves differently from a workload made

00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:01.340
of short prompts and long answers.

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:03.780
Long prompts stress prefill and context memory.

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:06.620
Long responses stress decode and sustained generation.

00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:09.040
Good tuning begins by naming the workload clearly,

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:12.805
because a batch configuration is only good relative to the kind of requests the system

00:01:12.805 --> 00:01:14.160
actually needs to serve.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:15.800
When reviewing benchmark output,

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:19.166
avoid collapsing prefill and decode into one vague speed number.

00:01:19.166 --> 00:01:23.100
A configuration can be excellent at absorbing a long prompt but ordinary during

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:23.700
generation,

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:24.660
or the other way around.

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:28.250
Separate measurements help you decide whether to tune for faster first response,

00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:31.700
smoother streaming, or better total capacity under repeated requests.
